#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/id #uuid "258b1f90-1c19-5a19-b796-a7c1c894bf17" :question/math-academy-id "e-5673" :question/problem "In a horse race with $8$ horses, how many different ways can the gold, silver, and bronze medals be awarded?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $8$ horses, so $n=8.$ We want to select $3$ of those horses for gold, silver, and bronze, so we have $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the medals is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{8}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{8!}{(8-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =336.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/id #uuid "5431e96c-87d1-517b-a565-9621a0b67913" :question/math-academy-id "e-5674" :question/problem "A company has $20$ employees. A total of $4$ employees are needed to fill the roles of president, vice president, treasurer, and secretary. How many different ways can this be achieved?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ employees, so $n=20.$ We want to select $4$ of those employees to fill the roles of president, vice president, treasurer, and secretary, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to fill the roles is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{20}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{20!}{(20-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20!}{16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17\\cdot \\cancel{16!}}{\\cancel{16!}} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =116\\,280.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "c72ffaba-7a68-5070-920d-fe241be5c797" :question/math-academy-id "e-5675" :question/problem "Evaluate $P(9,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_nP_r$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r  = \\dfrac {n!} {(n - r)!} \\,.\n$$\n\n$P(9,3)$ means the same thing as $_{9}P_3.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_nP_r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9,3) & =\\frac{9!}{(9-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =504.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation"}] :question/id #uuid "a18e8e9a-da60-5a42-840a-60f83137c6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7457" :question/problem "Calculate the value of ${}_{11}P_2.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_nP_r$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r  = \\dfrac {n!} {(n - r)!} \\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n={11}$ and $r=2$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n        \\eqalign{\n        \t\n        \t{}_{11}P_2  &= \\dfrac {11!} {(11 - 2)!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {11!}{9!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {11 \\cdot 10 \\cdot  \\cancel{9!}} {\\cancel{9!}} \\[5pt]\n                            &= 11 \\cdot 10 \\[5pt]\n                            &= 110.\n         }\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b1c87d-2bb9-5316-8b06-ba2ad49aa8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253958} {:answer/id #uuid "74455ded-e565-5af1-b02c-a8c28c379286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254698} {:answer/id #uuid "790a3132-0dff-5b56-91d0-b735d0135954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254739} {:answer/id #uuid "bc147852-b95c-5204-bc93-92c0051c947e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255351} {:answer/id #uuid "faeadc31-ea88-531c-8ec8-d365f63cad73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc147852-b95c-5204-bc93-92c0051c947e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb0b5c8-e9d4-5d72-af7f-b7fb4f615543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c859ea09-b109-5d77-bc6f-da4a3bb582e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122218" :question/problem "Evaluate $P(8,7).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\n$P(8,7)$ means the same thing as ${}_{8}{P}_{7}.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8,7) & =\\frac{8!}{(8-7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8!}{1!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =40\\,320.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2853cc-b929-563c-ab37-127998fea8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330323} {:answer/id #uuid "46073b0d-04c8-5f68-a136-3750790475c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330578} {:answer/id #uuid "674a56ce-a177-5893-9629-8e43fb7ba095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330876} {:answer/id #uuid "7c819272-39a8-5bdb-9108-2d80768e8184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331045} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b002a3-d2a1-535e-832b-a0d846b3bef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46073b0d-04c8-5f68-a136-3750790475c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330578} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dc1eb51-2850-5da0-8db3-0ded34dab401" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1406bce6-76e7-5954-aca6-0e4b86d8b417" :question/math-academy-id "q-5375" :question/problem "Evaluate $P(5,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\n$P(5,3)$ means the same thing as ${}_{5}{P}_{3}.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5,3) & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee6fa55-165b-5124-8c0e-3f7b5d787026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330216} {:answer/id #uuid "80cafd9f-57ad-5f52-adbd-a7510f74b7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331090} {:answer/id #uuid "a010d027-389b-5950-ac05-e99b96cfda08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331389} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1f99f3-2cfd-59a7-a4f0-bdc85f2ef347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331593} {:answer/id #uuid "cfca8b71-ca69-5d5b-8e52-79fd0e8029c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80cafd9f-57ad-5f52-adbd-a7510f74b7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331090} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dffd194-df88-5e19-91b6-bcea25a1c6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdbad7c7-dae3-5567-865d-d9e1a1c8b99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-5558" :question/problem "How many different arrangements of gold, silver, and bronze medalists can there be out of $10$ Olympic athletes, assuming there are no ties?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ athletes, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those athletes to receive gold, silver, or bronze medals, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the medals is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =720.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0b29e3-21ec-54f9-be80-6e8bf4e144c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330045} {:answer/id #uuid "16e41112-b299-5c3d-b9df-f8e3af77587c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330145} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb652e9-5a78-5db5-bed2-525d44c39f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answ